<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Can I Learn from This Experience? – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn from This Experience?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the main ideas of spoken news about familiar topics. Interactive: Contribute to simple discussions on a variety of issues.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the main ideas in spoken news reports or classroom stories about familiar learning experiences by answering comprehension questions (e.g., "What did Maria learn from her mistake?" "She should ask for help."). Can follow and contribute to discussions on how to improve study habits by sharing past experiences, making suggestions, and describing future actions (e.g., "What did you learn from your mistake?" "I forgot to review my notes." "What will you do next time?" "I will start studying earlier."). Can respond to questions in a discussion about learning experiences by recognizing suggestions (e.g., "What advice was given?" "I should practice every day.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a short audio clip (30-45 seconds) of a student describing a minor school mistake, for example, forgetting a deadline or an important item for `homework`. Students listen and try to identify the `mistake`. Examples: "I forgot my `homework`." or "I didn't `study` enough for the test." The teacher asks: "What `mistake` did the student make?" (Listening for gist).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher replays the audio clip and models the pronunciation of key vocabulary related to learning and mistakes, such as `mistakes`, `learn`, `study`, `improve`. The teacher also models the pronunciation of past tense verbs, focusing on `ed` endings: /d/ in "improved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "practiced." The teacher writes the words on the board and gestures to indicate the sound changes. E.g., "I `learned` (`/d/`) from my `mistake`." or "I `practiced` (`/t/`) English yesterday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up and new vocabulary using examples and questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is a 'mistake' a good thing or a bad thing?" (Expected answer: "A bad thing.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If you 'learn' from a mistake, do you repeat it?" (Expected answer: "No, you don't.")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also reviews minimal pair sounds relevant for listening comprehension, e.g., by saying "day" and "they" and asking students to identify the difference, to activate phonemic awareness for /d/ vs /ð/.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short audio story (1-1.5 minutes) about a student named Sofia who faced a `challenging` project and initially received low `grades` because of poor `study habits`. The audio describes how Sofia sought `feedback`, `improved` her `habits`, and achieved `better` `grades`. The narrative incorporates target vocabulary (`grades`, `study`, `habits`, `feedback`, `progress`, `improvement`, `success`, `practice`, `improve`, `try`, `learn`, `better`, `successful`, `challenging`, `carefully`, `well`) and grammar (past simple, future tense, `should` for advice). For example: "Sofia `learned` a lot from her `mistake`. She `realized` she `should` `study` more `carefully`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying the main ideas of spoken news or classroom stories about familiar topics (e.g., academic challenges, improving habits).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio story twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What was Sofia's main problem at first?" and "What was the main thing she `learned`?" (Students answer briefly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False & Vocabulary check):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements or questions for students to answer based on specific details from the audio. Examples: "Sofia always had good `grades`." (False) "She `improved` her `study habits`." (True) "What advice did her teacher give her?" (Listening for advice with "should"). The teacher ensures students recognize minimal pairs if they appear in the story (e.g., words with /s/ vs /θ/ like `sink` vs `think`).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying main ideas and advice from spoken input; using past simple to share experiences; using `should` for advice; future tense for improvements; target vocabulary (`mistakes`, `grades`, `study`, `habits`, `feedback`, `progress`, `improvement`, `success`, `practice`, `improve`, `try`, `learn`, `better`, `successful`, `challenging`, `carefully`, `well`). Pronunciation of `ed` verbs (/d/, /ɪd/, /t/) and recognition of minimal pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Listening Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> The teacher plays 5-6 short sentences (one at a time) related to the theme. Each sentence has a blank where a target vocabulary word or a form of `should` or past simple verb should be. Students listen `carefully` and write the missing word(s). Example audio: "I _____ to `study` harder after my `mistake`." (learned). "You _____ ask for `feedback`." (should). The teacher repeats sentences if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Listening & Repetition (Pronunciation Focus):</b> The teacher plays short phrases or sentences from the audio story. Students listen and repeat, paying close attention to the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in `ed`. The teacher highlights and corrects as needed. Example: "She `improved` her `grades`." (Focus on `/d/` in improved). "She `wanted` to do `better`." (Focus on `/ɪd/` in wanted). "She `practiced` `well`." (Focus on `/t/` in practiced). The teacher also provides pairs of words for students to listen and differentiate, e.g., "van" vs "ban" to practice /v/ vs /b/ sounds for comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Listen and Advise (Pair Work):</b> In pairs, Student A describes a simple past mistake (e.g., "Last week, I forgot to `study` for my history test."), using the past simple. Student B listens `carefully` and then gives advice using "You `should`..." and suggests a future action using "You `will`...". (e.g., "You `should` make a `study` schedule. Next time, you `will` remember to prepare `better`.") Students switch roles. The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension and use of target grammar/vocabulary.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Paired Discussion / Role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "What Can I Learn from This Experience?" and Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</b> Students will engage in a short conversation about a recent academic challenge or `mistake` and discuss how to `improve`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a card with a scenario (e.g., "You submitted a project with many `mistakes` and got low `grades`." or "You have poor `study habits` and your `progress` is slow.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A describes their `challenging` experience (based on the card or a real experience) using the past simple: "I had a `challenging` project last month. I didn't `study` enough, and my `grades` were not `good`." Student B actively listens to understand the main `mistake` and asks clarifying questions (e.g., "What `mistake` did you make?", "What `did you learn` from that experience?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A responds, explaining what they `learned` ("I `learned` that I `should` `practice` more `carefully`.") and what they `will` do `next time` ("Next time, I `will study` earlier.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B then offers constructive `feedback` and gives advice using "You `should`..." and suggests future `improvement` using "You `will`...". (e.g., "You `should` ask for `feedback` from your teacher. I think you `will` see `improvement` in your `grades`.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs swap roles and repeat the discussion with a new scenario or their partner's experience. The teacher monitors for fluency, active listening, and appropriate use of target language and `ed` pronunciation.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the Performance/Production stage to assess students' listening comprehension and their ability to contribute to discussions about `learning from mistakes`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main idea of partner's spoken problem.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks relevant questions about the `mistake` and `learning experience`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provides clear advice using "should" and plans for `improvement` using the future tense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (`mistakes`, `grades`, `study`, `habits`, `improve`, `feedback`, `learn`) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces `ed` verb endings (`improved`, `practiced`, `wanted`) with reasonable accuracy.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One new word I `learned` today from listening was...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One piece of advice I heard today that `I should` use is...</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was the most `challenging` part of listening to your classmates today? How can you `improve` your listening `skills`?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What is one specific `study habit` you `will` `try` to `improve` based on today's lesson?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening focus was successful in engaging students with the theme. Students were able to identify main ideas from audio and give simple advice. The pronunciation practice for `ed` endings (`improved`, `wanted`, `practiced`) needs continued reinforcement. Some students struggled with differentiating minimal pair sounds, indicating a need for more explicit phonemic awareness activities in future lessons. I will adjust for next time by providing more visual aids for new vocabulary and ensuring audio clips have clearer articulation for key words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students `will` build on the vocabulary and concepts introduced today by reading a short text or article related to `learning from mistakes` and `improving study habits`, focusing on reading comprehension strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>